==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|ēlevātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|[[elevo|ēlevāre]]||to raise, lift up}}, from {{m|la|ē-||out}} + {{m|la|[[levo|levāre]]||to make light, to lift}}, from {{m|la|levis||light}}; see [[levity]] and [[lever]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛləveɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-elevate.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[raise]] (something) to a [[higher]] [[position]].
#: {{syn|en|lift|raise}}
#: {{ant|en|drop|lower}}
#: {{ux|en|The doctor told me '''elevating''' my legs would help reduce the swelling.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1534|author={{w|William Marshall (translator)|William Marshall}}|author2={{w|George Joye}}|title=A Prymer in Englyshe|location=London|publisher=William Marshall|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A05789.0001.001
|passage=The Grace or Blessynge of the table to be sayed of chyldren standynge before it, thyr handes '''eleuated''' and [[joined|ioyned]] [[together|to gyder]]}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=V|scene=ii|passage=She had one eye declined for the loss of her husband, another '''elevated''' that the oracle was fulfilled: {{...}}}}
#* '''1750''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, ''{{w|The Rambler}},'' No.{{nbsp}}25, 12{{nbsp}}June, 1750, Volume{{nbsp}}1, London: J. Payne and J. Bouquet, 1752, p.{{nbsp}}216,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004772607.0001.001]</sup>
#*: We know that a few strokes of the axe will lop a cedar; but what arts of cultivation can '''elevate''' a shrub?
#* {{RQ:Conrad Outcast|chapter=V|page=138|passage=Abdulla expressed his surprise by '''elevating''' his eyebrows.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[promote]] (someone) to a higher [[rank]].
#: {{syn|en|exalt|promote}}
#: {{ant|en|demote}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1682|author={{w|Aphra Behn}}|chapter={{w|The Roundheads or, The Good Old Cause}}|location=London|publisher=D. Brown|title=et al.|section=Act I, Scene 1, p. 6|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A27320.0001.001
|passage=Hard Fate of Greatness, We so highly '''Elevated'''<br>Are more expos’d to Censure than the little ones,}}
#* {{RQ:Wollstonecraft Vindication Women|chapter=1|passage=Nothing can set the regal character in a more contemptible point of view, than the various crimes that have '''elevated''' men to the supreme dignity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author={{w|Joseph Heller}}|title={{w|Catch-22}}|location=New York|publisher=Dell|chapter=29|page=334|url=https://archive.org/details/catch19612200hell
|passage={{...}} that’s the way things go when you '''elevate''' mediocre people to positions of authority.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=A. D. Wright|title=The Early Modern Papacy
|passage=Much has also been made recently of the distorting effects exerted on the administration of Urban VIII by the interests of the Barberini nephews, especially of the two '''elevated''' to cardinal status.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Guy W. Lecky-Thompson|title=Inside SharePoint 2007 Administration|page=55
|passage=At that point, you have to '''elevate''' the account's rights, activate the feature, and then demote the account again.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[confer]] [[honor]] or [[nobility]] on (someone).
#: {{syn|en|ennoble|exalt|honor}}
#: {{ux|en|The traditional worldview '''elevates''' man as the pinnacle of creation.}}
#* '''1591''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, “Virgils Gnat” in ''Complaints,'' London: William Ponsonbie,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A12774.0001.001]</sup>
#*: That none, whom fortune freely doth aduaunce,
#*: Himselfe therefore to heauen should '''eleuate''':
#*: For loftie type of honour through the glaunce
#*: Of enuies dart, is downe in dust prostrate;
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make (something or someone) more [[worthy]] or of greater [[value]].
#: {{ux|en|A talented chef can '''elevate''' everyday ingredients into gourmet delights.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1682|author={{w|John Dryden}}|title=The Medal|location=Edinburgh|chapter=Epistle to the Whigs|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A36648.0001.001
|passage={{...}} if you encourage a young Beginner, who knows but he may '''elevate''' his stile a little,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1768|author={{w|William Gilpin}}|title=An Essay upon Prints|location=London|publisher=J. Robson|chapter=1|page=33|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004859666.0001.000
|passage=He is the true artist, who copies nature; but, where he finds her mean, '''elevates''' her from his own ideas of beauty.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens David Copperfield|volume=|chapter=|page=|passage=You can’t think how it '''elevates''' him in my opinion, to know for certain that he’s really conscientious!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[direct]] (the [[mind]], [[thought]]s, etc.) toward more worthy things.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1665|author={{w|Robert Boyle}}|title=Occasional Reflections upon Several Subjects|location=London|publisher=Henry Herringman|section=Section 4, Chapter 4, pp. 73-74|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A29010.0001.001
|passage={{...}} the devout Christian improves the Blessings he receives of this inferiour World, to '''elevate''' his mind above it:}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author={{w|Ahdaf Soueif}}|title=The Map of Love|location=New York|publisher=Anchor Books|year_published=2000|chapter=18|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=U8zhW9KiI8kC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=On the whole I would regard serious art as a means to '''elevate''' the emotions and educate the spirit {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[increase]] the [[intensity]] or [[degree]] of (something).
#: {{syn|en|increase|raise}}
#: {{ant|en|decrease|diminish|lower|reduce}}
#: {{ux|en|Some drugs have the [[side effect]] of '''elevating''' your blood sugar level.}}
## {{lb|en|dated}} To increase the [[loudness]] of (a sound, especially one's voice).
##* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|5|14|10|191|url=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015056795340&view=1up&seq=200|text={{...}} the Uncle had more than once '''elevated''' his Voice, so as to be heard down Stairs;}}
##* {{RQ:London Sea-Wolf|chapter=3|page=20|passage=“We’ll see to that,” Wolf Larsen answered, and '''elevated''' his voice in a call of “Cooky!”}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To lift the [[spirit]]s of (someone)
#: {{syn|en|cheer up|elate}}
#: {{ant|en|depress|sadden}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|8|lines=633-634|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A50919.0001.001|text={{...}} Hope '''elevates''', and joy<br>Bright’ns his Crest,}}
#* '''1759''', {{w|Adam Smith}}, ''{{w|The Theory of Moral Sentiments}},'' Edinburgh: A. Kincaid and J. Bell, Part{{nbsp}}1, Section{{nbsp}}2, Chapter{{nbsp}}1, p.{{nbsp}}20,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004894986.0001.000]</sup>
#*: It gives us the [[spleen]] {{...}} to see another too happy or too much '''elevated''', as we call it, with any little piece of good fortune.
# {{lb|en|dated|colloquial|humorous}} To [[intoxicate]] in a slight degree; to make (someone) [[tipsy]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=23 October 1755|author={{w|George Colman the Elder|George Colman}}|author2={{w|Bonnell Thornton}}|journal={{w|The Connoisseur (newspaper)|The Connoisseur}}|number=91|volume=2|location=London|publisher=R. Baldwin|year_published=1756|page=557|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004813445.0001.002
|passage=''{{w|Richard Steele|Steele}}'' entertained them till he was tipsy; when the same wine that stupified him, only served to '''elevate''' ''{{w|Joseph Addison|Addison}},'' who took up the ball just as ''Steele'' dropt it, and kept it up for the rest of the evening.}}
#* {{RQ:Boswell Johnson|volume=2|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004839390.0001.002|text=[Johnson,] from drinking only water, supposed every body who drank wine to be '''elevated'''}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|volume=1|chapter=3|page=92|url=https://archive.org/details/peverilpeak01unkngoog/page/n132|text={{...}} the '''elevated''' Cavaliers {{...}} sent to Roger Raine of the Peveril Arms {{...}} for two tubs of merry [[stingo]]}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|[[Latinism]]}} To attempt to make (something) seem less [[important]], [[remarkable]], etc.
#: {{syn|en|lessen|detract|disparage}}
#* '''1660''', {{w|Jeremy Taylor}}, ''Ductor Dubitantium,'' London: Richard Royston, Volume{{nbsp}}1, Chapter{{nbsp}}4, Rule{{nbsp}}2, p.{{nbsp}}126,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A63844.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} the Arabian Physicians {{...}} endevour to '''elevate''' and lessen the thing [''i.e.'' belief in the {{w|virgin birth of Jesus}}], by saying, It is not wholly beyond the force of nature, that a Virgin should conceive {{...}}

